A||||2||Shiner||Non-Fiero Street Racing Story||||6||
Z||000000||Shiner||10-20-2000||10:00 PM||||I guess this belongs in this section.<BR>Well, my father was on his bike, a '96 Honda VFR750 Interceptor, cruising along on a local scenic route, which is very flat and has great visibility.  There was a slow car in front of him, so he put his blinker on and waited for the broken line to come up, which was about 100 yards.  Out of nowhere, a Ducati 900 SuperSport of the same vintage roars by, passing the car ahead and continuing down the road at full throttle.  I guess this provoked my father, as he dropped the Honda down two or three gears and wound it out.  He caught up to the Duc at the end of fifth gear, and banged it up into sixth.  As soon as it came up on cam, he ripped past the Duc, doing somewhere over a buck thirty.  <P> I know you guys love underdog stories, so there is a good one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000001||Raydar||10-20-2000||11:55 PM||||The VFR 750 is a great bike. If I was still into bikes, I'd really consider buying one. It would be a toss up between that or an FJ 1200.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||14
Z||000002||Shiner||10-21-2000||01:55 AM||||Yeah, it really is a nice bike.  It's reliability is almost erie  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I REALLY like Ducatis though.  I would spring for a 900 Monster or SS, but the dry clutch thing really stinks.  Who the hell wants to replace their clutch every six thousand miles?!?!  And the parts prices are stunning, but that goes without saying.||||reg||1
